     which means that if a regular expression matches another defined mount point,
     it will override the previously defined threshold.
 
+    It is also possible to alert when usage drops *below* an expected level,
+    which usually indicates that a filesystem failed to mount (so what looks
+    like the mount point is really just an empty directory on the root
+    filesystem) rather than that space was genuinely freed up. To do this,
+    pass a mapping with ``above`` and/or ``below`` keys instead of a bare
+    threshold.
+
+    .. code-block:: yaml
+
+        beacons:
+          diskusage:
+            - /mnt/nfs:
+                above: 90%
+                below: 5%
+
+    ``above`` behaves exactly like the plain threshold form above. ``below``
+    is optional and independent of it: a mount can be configured with only
+    ``above``, only ``below``, or both.
+
     """
     whitelist = []
     config = salt.utils.beacons.remove_hidden_options(config, whitelist)
